1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
Imagine you're living 50,000 years ago.

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:11,000
No courts, no judges, no lawyers billing you by the hour for services rendered in language you don't fully understand. No

3
00:00:11,000 --> 00:00:18,000
prisons, no police departments, no municipal code violation notices left passive aggressively on your door while

4
00:00:18,000 --> 00:00:27,000
you were out running errands. No institution of any kind dedicated to the formal organized state sanctioned administration of justice. and someone

5
00:00:27,000 --> 00:00:36,000
in your group just stole your food, or attacked someone weaker without provocation, or kept taking more than their share at every single communal

6
00:00:36,000 --> 00:00:44,000
meal while everyone else quietly seethed and said nothing individually because nobody wanted to be the one to start something. Or they started behaving in

7
00:00:44,000 --> 00:00:52,000
ways that made the rest of the group feel genuinely, persistently unsafe, erratic, violent, unpredictable. The

8
00:00:52,000 --> 00:01:02,000
kind of person whose presence transforms the social atmosphere of a small band from cooperative and functional into tense, watchful, and quietly exhausted.

9
00:01:02,000 --> 00:01:10,000
What do you do? This is not a rhetorical question designed to make you feel grateful for modern institutions. It's one of the most important practical

10
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:18,000
problems ancient humans faced continuously across hundreds of thousands of years of existence. And they solved it not with stone tablets,

11
00:01:18,000 --> 00:01:25,000
not with written law, not with any formal institution we would recognize as a justice system today. They solved it

12
00:01:25,000 --> 00:01:32,000
with something older, more flexible, more socially intelligent, and in some ways more genuinely egalitarian than

13
00:01:32,000 --> 00:01:40,000
anything that came after it. And the way they solved it tells us something profound about where every legal system that followed eventually came from.

14
00:01:40,000 --> 00:01:46,000
Because here is the thing most people have never been told. The impulse to punish is not a product of civilization.

15
00:01:46,000 --> 00:01:54,000
The machinery for social justice didn't arrive with cities and kings and written codes carved into polished stone pillars. It was running in a

16
00:01:54,000 --> 00:02:01,000
recognizably functional and remarkably sophisticated form in small bands of hunter gatherers long before any of that

17
00:02:01,000 --> 00:02:10,000
existed. Humans were building justice systems before they built walls. Let's go through exactly how it worked and then what happened when civilization

18
00:02:10,000 --> 00:02:18,000
arrived and decided to formalize everything. First, you have to understand the social context of pre-aggricultural life because the

19
00:02:18,000 --> 00:02:25,000
context changes everything about what punishment meant, how it operated, and why it took the specific forms it took.

20
00:02:25,000 --> 00:02:33,000
Pre-aggricultural hunter gatherers. The people we're talking about when we say ancient humans in this context lived in

21
00:02:33,000 --> 00:02:38,000
small, highly mobile bands of typically 20 to 50 individuals.

22
00:02:38,000 --> 00:02:47,000
Everybody knew everybody. You couldn't hide your behavior for long. You couldn't develop a carefully cultivated reputation as a generous and trustworthy

23
00:02:47,000 --> 00:02:55,000
person in one part of town while quietly behaving very differently somewhere else. Because there was no other part of town. The community was the entire

24
00:02:55,000 --> 00:03:03,000
social world and it was observing everything you did continuously. Because in a group that size, there is effectively no private behavior that the

25
00:03:03,000 --> 00:03:12,000
group doesn't eventually learn about through proximity, observation, and the oldest information network in human history, which is gossip, but we'll get

26
00:03:12,000 --> 00:03:20,000
to that. This created a social environment radically different from modern anonymous urban life. In one critical way, your reputation was not

27
00:03:20,000 --> 00:03:30,000
merely socially important. It was existential in the most literal sense of the word. Your survival, your access to shared resources, your ability to form

28
00:03:30,000 --> 00:03:38,000
alliances that would matter during illness or injury or conflict, your reproductive prospects, your ability to call on collective help when you were

29
00:03:38,000 --> 00:03:43,000
vulnerable. All of it flowed directly from your standing within the group.

30
00:03:43,000 --> 00:03:52,000
Destroy your reputation and you didn't lose social status in the way a modern person loses social status. You lost access to everything the group made

31
00:03:52,000 --> 00:04:00,000
possible, which in a paleolithic environment was essentially everything that separated you from an extremely difficult solo existence in a landscape

32
00:04:00,000 --> 00:04:08,000
full of predators, unpredictable food availability, and no institutional safety net of any description. This is

33
00:04:08,000 --> 00:04:12,000
the foundation on which the entire pre-aggricultural justice system rested.

34
00:04:12,000 --> 00:04:20,000
Not law, not authority, not force applied by designated officials. The foundation was interdependence and the

35
00:04:20,000 --> 00:04:28,000
awareness of interdependence and the understanding shared, implicit, never needing to be written down, that your

36
00:04:28,000 --> 00:04:36,000
continued membership in good standing within this specific group of people was the single most important asset you possessed. Anthropologist Christopher

37
00:04:36,000 --> 00:04:45,000
Bow spent decades studying hunter gatherer societies across every inhabited continent and documented their social control mechanisms in landmark

38
00:04:45,000 --> 00:04:53,000
works. most notably his book Hierarchy in the Forest published by Harvard University Press. His conclusion reached

39
00:04:53,000 --> 00:05:03,000
after systematically analyzing data from 48 distinct human societies representing the full geographic range of human hunter gatherer experience was both

40
00:05:03,000 --> 00:05:10,000
elegant and genuinely counterintuitive to modern assumptions. Hunter gatherer societies were not anarchctic. They did

41
00:05:10,000 --> 00:05:20,000
not operate on pure individual survival instinct with no social regulation. They were intensely, deliberately, collectively egalitarian.

42
00:05:20,000 --> 00:05:29,000
And they maintained that egalitarianism through a sophisticated multi-stage system of social enforcement that operated without any formal institution,

43
00:05:29,000 --> 00:05:37,000
designated authority or written rule of any kind. Bow called it the reverse dominance hierarchy. And the name

44
00:05:37,000 --> 00:05:45,000
captures something important. In most primate societies, chimpanzees, gorillas, baboons, the powerful dominate

45
00:05:45,000 --> 00:05:51,000
the weak. Alphas take the best food, the best mates, the best resting positions.

46
00:05:51,000 --> 00:05:58,000
Rank is established through strength, maintained through intimidation, and the bottom of the hierarchy submits because

47
00:05:58,000 --> 00:06:07,000
the top can credibly hurt it. Power flows downward. Hunter gatherers flipped this entirely. In band societies, the

48
00:06:07,000 --> 00:06:13,000
collective, the group as a unified social entity, dominated the individual.

49
00:06:13,000 --> 00:06:20,000
If anyone started accumulating more than their share of food, more status than the group was comfortable with, more influence over decisions than a peer

50
00:06:20,000 --> 00:06:28,000
among equals was supposed to have, the group closed ranks against them. not through any formal process, through

51
00:06:28,000 --> 00:06:37,000
coordinated, sustained, escalating social pressure that started soft and became progressively harder until the problem was either resolved or removed.

52
00:06:37,000 --> 00:06:45,000
The system had stages, clear, consistent, documented stages, appearing with striking similarity across cultures

53
00:06:45,000 --> 00:06:52,000
that never had any contact with each other. The first stage was gossip. And before you dismiss that as trivial, hear

54
00:06:52,000 --> 00:07:01,000
out what gossip actually does in this context because it is doing serious work. In the huntergatherer setting, gossip is not idle social entertainment.

55
00:07:01,000 --> 00:07:07,000
It is a powerful, functionally essential tool for collective moral enforcement.

56
00:07:07,000 --> 00:07:14,000
When someone began behaving in ways the community found unacceptable, hoarding food, bullying weaker members, cheating

57
00:07:14,000 --> 00:07:22,000
at sharing, free riding on the labor of others while contributing substantially less than expected. The community started talking about it, not to the

58
00:07:22,000 --> 00:07:30,000
offender's face initially, among themselves. Building shared understanding that this specific person's specific behavior was a

59
00:07:30,000 --> 00:07:38,000
specific problem. developing consensus, coordinating a collective interpretation of events before any public action was

60
00:07:38,000 --> 00:07:46,000
taken, so that when public action did arrive, it arrived with the full weight of unanimous community opinion rather than as an individual accusation that

61
00:07:46,000 --> 00:07:51,000
the offender could dismiss or retaliate against personally.

62
00:07:51,000 --> 00:07:58,000
Anthropological research on modern huntergatherer populations, the Dutch Kungan of the Kalahari, the Hadza of

63
00:07:58,000 --> 00:08:06,000
Tanzania, the Jew/Hoansi, multiple Aboriginal Australian groups consistently finds gossip serving

64
00:08:06,000 --> 00:08:15,000
precisely this regulatory function. It is the early warning system, the community newsletter, the consensus building mechanism, and the social

65
00:08:15,000 --> 00:08:20,000
verdict delivery system all operating simultaneously in casual conversation.

66
00:08:20,000 --> 00:08:28,000
Before any formal sanction is applied in these communities, the community has already reached an informal distributed verdict through thousands of small

67
00:08:28,000 --> 00:08:35,000
conversations that together constitute a kind of collective social trial conducted without any designated venue,

68
00:08:35,000 --> 00:08:40,000
judge, or procedure. The offender usually knows the gossip is happening.

69
00:08:40,000 --> 00:08:48,000
In a group of 30 people living in constant proximity, you can feel the shift in social temperature, the conversations that stop when you

70
00:08:48,000 --> 00:08:56,000
approach, the sharing that happens slightly less fluidly, the eye contact that is calibrated differently. And frequently at this stage alone, before

71
00:08:56,000 --> 00:09:04,000
anything more formal occurs, the offender changes their behavior because they understand without anyone spelling

72
00:09:04,000 --> 00:09:11,000
it out explicitly what the next stages look like and what the end point of the process is. if they don't correct

73
00:09:11,000 --> 00:09:19,000
course. This is deterrence without a criminal code. It's working through reputation mechanics that are older than language itself. If gossip failed to

74
00:09:19,000 --> 00:09:28,000
produce the necessary correction, the second stage was public criticism and ridicule delivered openly in front of the entire group. In a small band

75
00:09:28,000 --> 00:09:37,000
society, public ridicule is not a minor social discomfort that you survive and eventually forget about. It is a serious social event with lasting structural

76
00:09:37,000 --> 00:09:46,000
consequences. When the community decides to confront an offender's behavior openly, naming it, mocking it, calling it out explicitly in a context where

77
00:09:46,000 --> 00:09:52,000
everyone present understands exactly what is happening, the target is receiving a very precise communication.

78
00:09:52,000 --> 00:10:01,000
The informal protection and goodwill the group normally extends to its members has been formally suspended for you, pending behavioral correction. Your

79
00:10:01,000 --> 00:10:08,000
status is being publicly revised downward in a way that everyone witnesses and that becomes part of the community's shared understanding of who

80
00:10:08,000 --> 00:10:17,000
you are. Bow's research found this pattern with striking consistency across cultures separated by thousands of miles and thousands of years. The Inuit of the

81
00:10:17,000 --> 00:10:25,000
Arctic, the Akangan of Southern Africa, and multiple Aboriginal Australian groups all maintained active traditions

82
00:10:25,000 --> 00:10:32,000
of public criticism and ridicule aimed specifically at individuals who hoarded resources, attempted to dominate social

83
00:10:32,000 --> 00:10:40,000
decisions, bullied others, or refused to participate in the reciprocal sharing that was the economic foundation of band

84
00:10:40,000 --> 00:10:45,000
life. The specific forms varied. The function was identical.

85
00:10:45,000 --> 00:10:53,000
A particularly striking version of this that appears repeatedly across cultures is what anthropologists call leveling mechanisms.

86
00:10:53,000 --> 00:11:01,000
Communal practices designed with explicit intent to prevent any individual from accumulating more status, resources, or social authority

87
00:11:01,000 --> 00:11:08,000
than the community's egalitarian norms permitted. In multiple documented huntergatherer cultures, a successful

88
00:11:08,000 --> 00:11:17,000
hunter was not expected to announce or celebrate his own achievement. a man who returned to camp and loudly proclaimed that he had made a remarkable kill, that

89
00:11:17,000 --> 00:11:25,000
he had been especially skilled, that the group should recognize his contribution, that man was considered borish, socially

90
00:11:25,000 --> 00:11:32,000
clumsy, and potentially dangerous in his apparent appetite for status. He would be mocked. The kill was real, the food

91
00:11:32,000 --> 00:11:41,000
was shared. Everyone ate it, but the social credit was distributed collectively rather than concentrated in the individual who produced it. If he

92
00:11:41,000 --> 00:11:50,000
pushed back against that distribution, if he insisted on receiving the recognition he felt he'd earned, the community pushed back harder because

93
00:11:50,000 --> 00:11:58,000
what looked like a dispute about credit was understood by everyone present as a dispute about power. And allowing one person to accumulate power beyond what

94
00:11:58,000 --> 00:12:07,000
the egalitarian structure permitted was understood as an existential threat to the social order that kept everyone safe. When public ridicule failed to

95
00:12:07,000 --> 00:12:11,000
produce correction, the third stage was ostracism.

96
00:12:11,000 --> 00:12:18,000
And this one had consequences that were in the ancient context genuinely severe.

97
00:12:18,000 --> 00:12:25,000
In a modern city, being shunned by a social group is painful and unpleasant, but ultimately survivable. There are

98
00:12:25,000 --> 00:12:34,000
other social options. You can move neighborhoods, join different communities, rebuild a social life from scratch. The city contains thousands of

99
00:12:34,000 --> 00:12:41,000
people you haven't met yet. The infrastructure of modern life, food availability, shelter, medical care

100
00:12:41,000 --> 00:12:48,000
doesn't depend on any specific group accepting you. In a small band of 20 to 50 people moving through a paleolithic

101
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:57,000
landscape with the nearest other human group potentially weeks of difficult travel away, being excluded from the community was not a social consequence

102
00:12:57,000 --> 00:13:06,000
in the modern sense. It was a death sentence with a variable delay delivered without anyone having to physically harm you. You couldn't hunt as effectively

103
00:13:06,000 --> 00:13:14,000
alone as a coordinated group could. You couldn't defend yourself against predators during sleep without others watching. You couldn't process large

104
00:13:14,000 --> 00:13:21,000
game, tend injuries, survive serious illness, carry infants, or maintain the hundred small cooperative tasks that

105
00:13:21,000 --> 00:13:28,000
group life made manageable and solo life made nearly impossible. Exclusion from the ban removed the cooperative

106
00:13:28,000 --> 00:13:33,000
infrastructure that made individual survival viable in harsh environments.

107
00:13:33,000 --> 00:13:42,000
It exposed every vulnerability that group membership had been quietly shielding. Ostracism in documented huntergatherer societies operated in

108
00:13:42,000 --> 00:13:46,000
degrees that match the escalating structure of the whole system.

109
00:13:46,000 --> 00:13:53,000
Initially, it might manifest as a subtle reduction in sharing. The offender remained technically included in group

110
00:13:53,000 --> 00:14:01,000
activities, but found that food and resources reached them slightly less smoothly than before, that allies were somehow less available when needed, that

111
00:14:01,000 --> 00:14:10,000
the social warmth that had previously surrounded them had been dialed down in ways that were real but individually deniable. If behavior continued, the

112
00:14:10,000 --> 00:14:19,000
exclusion formalized. In highly mobile groups, the community could simply move camp without the offender. A specific cruelty available only to people who

113
00:14:19,000 --> 00:14:26,000
move regularly and who control entirely whether any given individual knows where they've gone. Cases documented across

114
00:14:26,000 --> 00:14:35,000
multiple ethnographic sources involve persistent bullies, chronic resource thieves, and individuals who repeatedly violated sexual boundaries within the

115
00:14:35,000 --> 00:14:44,000
group. For these people, when ostracism was fully applied, the outcomes documented in the ethnographic record were typically one of two things.

116
00:14:44,000 --> 00:14:51,000
Behavioral correction in which the person demonstrated genuine change was cautiously reintegrated and was watched

117
00:14:51,000 --> 00:15:01,000
carefully afterward or death either from the practical vulnerabilities of isolation or from the escalation to the fourth and final stage.

118
00:15:01,000 --> 00:15:08,000
The fourth stage was execution. Hunter gatherer execution is one of the most consistently documented and most

119
00:15:08,000 --> 00:15:15,000
genuinely startling findings in the extensive ethnographic literature on band societies. Bow compiled execution

120
00:15:15,000 --> 00:15:24,000
cases from across dozens of hunter gatherer cultures on multiple continents and found the practice not only widespread but structurally consistent

121
00:15:24,000 --> 00:15:30,000
in ways that reveal a coherent underlying logic rather than random violence.

122
00:15:30,000 --> 00:15:38,000
when a member of the group had committed sufficiently serious offenses and critically had failed to respond to every lesser intervention the community

123
00:15:38,000 --> 00:15:46,000
had applied across multiple rounds of escalating pressure. The group collectively reached a consensus decision to kill them. Not a designated

124
00:15:46,000 --> 00:15:54,000
authority figure acting on institutional mandate, not a chief exercising unilateral power. the entire adult

125
00:15:54,000 --> 00:16:02,000
community reaching consensus through exactly the same distributed deliberation process that characterized every earlier stage and then a small

126
00:16:02,000 --> 00:16:09,000
number of individuals, typically men, carrying out the act on behalf of everyone simultaneously.

127
00:16:09,000 --> 00:16:17,000
The deliberately collective structure of the decision was not incidental. It was the whole point. An individual who killed another group member was a

128
00:16:17,000 --> 00:16:26,000
murderer and would be treated as one, potentially subjected to the same process he had just witnessed applied to someone else. A community that had

129
00:16:26,000 --> 00:16:34,000
collectively reached consensus that a member had become an intolerable threat to everyone and that had collectively decided to remove that threat was doing

130
00:16:34,000 --> 00:16:41,000
something categorically different from individual violence. The communal nature of the act removed it from the domain of

131
00:16:41,000 --> 00:16:50,000
personal grievance and placed it explicitly in the domain of social necessity. Responsibility was distributed so broadly that no single

132
00:16:50,000 --> 00:16:57,000
person bore the full weight of it. The offenses that drew this ultimate community response were consistent across cultures to a degree that is

133
00:16:57,000 --> 00:17:04,000
itself significant. unprovoked and repeated serious violence against group members, serial killing within the band,

134
00:17:04,000 --> 00:17:13,000
predatory sexual behavior that the individual had demonstrated across multiple rounds of lesser intervention was not going to stop. The threshold was

135
00:17:13,000 --> 00:17:22,000
not a single act in the heat of conflict. The pattern preceding community sanctioned execution was almost universally one of documented

136
00:17:22,000 --> 00:17:30,000
escalation. A person who had been warned, mocked, partially excluded, fully excluded, and who had responded to

137
00:17:30,000 --> 00:17:38,000
each stage by continuing or escalating precisely the behaviors that made them dangerous to everyone around them. This is in its essential structure

138
00:17:38,000 --> 00:17:47,000
recognizably a justice system, not a primitive proto version of one, not the blunt early sketch of something that would eventually become law, a justice

139
00:17:47,000 --> 00:17:52,000
system. It has proportionality. Minor offenses produce minor sanctions.

140
00:17:52,000 --> 00:18:00,000
Serious offenses escalate through progressively severe responses. It has process. The community doesn't jump from

141
00:18:00,000 --> 00:18:10,000
first offense to execution. It works methodically through stages with space for a correction at each level. It has consensus. No individual decides alone.

142
00:18:10,000 --> 00:18:18,000
It has purpose. The explicit goal is not personal revenge, but the restoration and maintenance of a functional, safe,

143
00:18:18,000 --> 00:18:27,000
cooperative social environment for everyone who depends on it. Now, fast forward several thousand years, because this is where the story takes a turn

144
00:18:27,000 --> 00:18:35,000
that complicates the simple narrative of human progress. When humans settled into agricultural societies and began building the first cities and formal

145
00:18:35,000 --> 00:18:39,000
states, the justice system didn't immediately improve in every dimension.

146
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:46,000
It got more elaborate. It got written down. It got scalable in ways that small band social enforcement could never bay.

147
00:18:46,000 --> 00:18:54,000
And it introduced something the hunter gatherer system, for all its limitations and occasional brutality, had been specifically and deliberately designed

148
00:18:54,000 --> 00:19:01,000
to prevent. The oldest physical law code that archaeologists have so far recovered is the code of Uramu, written

149
00:19:01,000 --> 00:19:10,000
in ancient Sumer in Mesopotamia around 2,100 to 250 B.CE, more than 4,000 years ago.

150
00:19:10,000 --> 00:19:18,000
It was inscribed on clay tablets in uniform script organized in a logical if then structure that any modern lawmaker

151
00:19:18,000 --> 00:19:28,000
would immediately recognize. If a person commits offense X, the legal consequence is Y. Clean, legible, consistent,

152
00:19:28,000 --> 00:19:37,000
written down so that it applied predictably to everyone rather than being administered differently in every individual case by whoever happened to be in charge that day.

153
00:19:37,000 --> 00:19:46,000
What's genuinely remarkable about Ernamu's code when you look at it closely is the approach it took to physical harm between ordinary people

154
00:19:46,000 --> 00:19:54,000
for bodily injury. Knocking out someone's eye, breaking someone's bone, knocking out a tooth. The code specified

155
00:19:54,000 --> 00:20:01,000
monetary fines rather than physical retaliation. A knocked out eye cost the offender half a mina of silver. A

156
00:20:01,000 --> 00:20:09,000
knocked out tooth cost two shekels of silver. The orientation was toward financial compensation for the victim rather than physical punishment

157
00:20:09,000 --> 00:20:16,000
inflicted on the offender. Restore the balance economically. Make the harmed party whole or as close to whole as

158
00:20:16,000 --> 00:20:25,000
money could manage. Murder, robbery, adultery, and rape were still capital offenses. But the general treatment of

159
00:20:25,000 --> 00:20:31,000
physical harm between ordinary people was, by the standards of what immediately followed it in the historical record, surprisingly

160
00:20:31,000 --> 00:20:38,000
measured. That measured approach didn't survive the next three centuries intact.

161
00:20:38,000 --> 00:20:47,000
Around 1750 B.CE, a Babylonian king named Hammurabi commissioned what became the most famous legal document in all of

162
00:20:47,000 --> 00:20:55,000
ancient history. [snorts] 282 laws carved into a black diorite pillar standing more than 7 ft tall. discovered

163
00:20:55,000 --> 00:21:06,000
by French archaeologists in 1901 in what is now Iran and now held in the Louvre in Paris where it remains one of the most visited artifacts in the museum.

164
00:21:06,000 --> 00:21:14,000
Hammurabi's code moved decisively and deliberately away from the compensation model of Uramu toward something the ancient world would become permanently

165
00:21:14,000 --> 00:21:23,000
associated with an eye for an eye, a tooth for a tooth. The principle is called lexalionus,

166
00:21:23,000 --> 00:21:30,000
the law of retaliation. And Hammurabi didn't invent the concept, but he elevated it to a formal prominence it

167
00:21:30,000 --> 00:21:34,000
had never previously achieved in written law.

168
00:21:34,000 --> 00:21:42,000
Under his code, the punishment was designed to mirror the crime physically and directly. If a builder constructed a house that collapsed and killed the

169
00:21:42,000 --> 00:21:50,000
owner's son, the builder's son would be executed, not the builder, his son. The

170
00:21:50,000 --> 00:21:58,000
logic of collective family accountability for individual crimes produced some of the code's most striking and to modern eyes most

171
00:21:58,000 --> 00:22:05,000
troubling provisions. But the element of Hammurabi's code that most sharply distinguishes it from everything that

172
00:22:05,000 --> 00:22:13,000
came before, including the hunter gatherer system Bow documented is its explicit formal builtin class

173
00:22:13,000 --> 00:22:21,000
stratification of punishment. If a free man knocked out the eye of another free man, the free man lost his eye. If the

174
00:22:21,000 --> 00:22:29,000
same free man knocked out the eye of a slave, he paid a fine. The physical injury in both cases was identical. The

175
00:22:29,000 --> 00:22:37,000
social status of the victim determined the entire legal response. The harm to the victim was the same. The law treated

176
00:22:37,000 --> 00:22:44,000
it as categorically different depending on who the victim was. This was genuinely new. This was what settled

177
00:22:44,000 --> 00:22:50,000
hierarchical institutionally governed society added to the justice equation.

178
00:22:50,000 --> 00:22:59,000
formal written consistently applied rules which represented a real advance over purely ad hoc individual decisions

179
00:22:59,000 --> 00:23:07,000
combined with explicit cotification of inequality into the structure of punishment itself. The Hunter Gatherer Band with its obsessive leveling

180
00:23:07,000 --> 00:23:16,000
mechanisms and its reverse dominance hierarchy and its deep collective suspicion of any individual accumulating disproportionate power or privilege

181
00:23:16,000 --> 00:23:24,000
would have recognized this instantly as precisely the thing it had been working so hard for so long to prevent.

182
00:23:24,000 --> 00:23:32,000
Bow documented how hunter gatherer communities actively resisted attempts by any member to claim special treatment, including specifically the

183
00:23:32,000 --> 00:23:40,000
claim that harm done to a more important person deserved a more serious response than harm done to a less important one.

184
00:23:40,000 --> 00:23:48,000
The whole reverse dominance hierarchy existed to prevent exactly that logic from taking hold. Civilization wrote that logic into law and called it

185
00:23:48,000 --> 00:23:57,000
justice. Here's what all of this adds up to, and it is genuinely worth sitting with for a moment. The huntergatherer justice system, with its escalating

186
00:23:57,000 --> 00:24:04,000
stages moving from gossip through ridicule, through ostracism, through community execution, running on collective consensus rather than

187
00:24:04,000 --> 00:24:11,000
designated authority, maintained by distributed social responsibility rather than institutional officials, was not a

188
00:24:11,000 --> 00:24:19,000
crude, undeveloped prototype of what came later. In several important respects, it was more sophisticated than the early written codes that replaced

189
00:24:19,000 --> 00:24:28,000
it. It had genuine proportionality built in from the ground up. It had multi-stage process with space for correction at every level. It had a

190
00:24:28,000 --> 00:24:35,000
distributional ethics encoded in its design. The powerful were not exempt from social sanction. The entire point

191
00:24:35,000 --> 00:24:42,000
of the reverse dominance hierarchy was that nobody was exempt. Being stronger or better at hunting or more charismatic

192
00:24:42,000 --> 00:24:50,000
bought you no protection whatsoever from communal enforcement of shared behavioral norms. If anything, accumulating any of those advantages

193
00:24:50,000 --> 00:25:01,000
made the community watch you more carefully, not less. The shift to written codes, formal institutions, and professional legal systems brought genuine irreplaceable benefits.

194
00:25:01,000 --> 00:25:10,000
Predictability across a much larger population. protection from purely arbitrary individual decisions by whoever happened to have power at any

195
00:25:10,000 --> 00:25:19,000
given moment. The ability to govern communities far too large for everyone to know everyone personally. But it also introduced the formalization of social

196
00:25:19,000 --> 00:25:27,000
inequality into the structure of justice itself in a way that the huntergatherer system had been actively and consciously fighting against for most of human

197
00:25:27,000 --> 00:25:34,000
existence. The ancient human standing in collective judgment over a chronic bully who had refused every lesser intervention the community offered

198
00:25:34,000 --> 00:25:42,000
didn't have a courtroom or a gavel or a legal code or a professional vocabulary for what they were doing. But they had

199
00:25:42,000 --> 00:25:51,000
something that every court in every civilization that followed was trying with varying degrees of success to replicate. The genuine consensus of a

200
00:25:51,000 --> 00:25:54,000
community that collectively cared about the outcome. Everything else, the codes, the courts, the carved stone pillars, the lawyers, was infrastructure.
